  "title": "Erdős Problem #1100",
  "statement": {
    "representation": "markdown-latex",
    "text": "If $1=d_1<\\cdots<d_{\\tau(n)}=n$ are the divisors of $n$, then let $\\tau_\\perp(n)$ count the number of $i$ for which $(d_i,d_{i+1})=1$.\nIs it true that $\\tau_\\perp(n)/\\omega(n)\\to \\infty$ for almost all $n$? Is it true that $ \\tau_\\perp(n)< \\exp((\\log n)^{o(1)}) $ for all $n$?\nLet $ g(k) = \\max_{\\omega(n)=k}\\tau_\\perp(n), $ where $\\omega(n)$ counts the number of distinct prime divisors of $n$, and $n$ is restricted to squarefree integers. Determine the growth of $g(k)$.",
